Z||000011||fieroshop||01-23-2001||11:11 AM||||Several things in answer to your questions.<BR>1. You should replace the O2 sensor with every tune-up.<BR>2. If you get an engine check light and it states that you have a bad O@, then it might be too late for the cat.<BR>3. Most of the toime when an O2 sensor goes out the catalytic converter also goes aout. The reason being is if the O2 sensor fails it reads lean. Which tells the ECM to dump more fuel and the results are that burns the cat real quick. I had a brand new cat burn up in 4 blocks, due to a bad O@ sensor. Had to replace the O2 and then the cat for a second time in one day. That was when I first opened shop and now we replace the O@ with every tune-up and have had no problems.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||01-23-2001||12:54 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by fieroshop:<BR>[B]Several things in answer to your questions.<BR>1. You should replace the O2 sensor with every tune-up.<P>That sounds good to me, most cars anymore only need tuneups every 50K or 100K. Factories have to warranty all emissions for 60K, so OX2 sensor should be covered under that? Dealer put 2 new cat converters on my Lincoln under that warranty at 90K for free.||||reg||1
